<p><%= l(:"reporting.help.intro") %></p>

<h3 id="contracts">Contracts</h3>
<p>A contract grants credit days to a project over its dates, for the time logged on issues of its trackers.
Any length works: a 6-month maintenance, then one-year contracts, then a 3-month one to align on a fiscal year.
A tracker belongs to one contract of the project at a time; two contracts can run side by side on different trackers.</p>
<p>A contract starts from zero on its first day and stops on its last one. Nothing carries over by itself: see
<a href="#renewal">Renewal</a>.</p>

<h3 id="envelopes">Envelopes</h3>
<p>The credit of a contract is split into envelopes. Each one has days, granted on the contract's first day, and
criteria: some activities (or <em>all other activities</em>) and which issues it takes (all, public only or private only).</p>
<p>Each logged hour is charged to one envelope at most:</p>
<ol>
  <li>the issue's tracker and the date select the contract;</li>
  <li>the activity and the issue's privacy select the envelope, listed activities first, then the <em>all other activities</em> envelope;</li>
  <li>an hour matching no envelope, or logged without an issue, is <strong>outside the contract</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<table class="list">
  <thead><tr><th>Envelope</th><th>Activities</th><th>Issues</th><th>Days</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Development</td><td>Design, Development, Tests</td><td>Public only</td><td>30</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Project management</td><td>Management</td><td>Private only</td><td>10</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>With these envelopes, development on a public issue goes to <em>Development</em>; the project manager's time on a
recurring private issue goes to <em>Project management</em>; development on a private issue (internal work on a test
environment, for instance) matches no envelope and is not charged to the client.</p>

<h3 id="movements">Refills and adjustments</h3>
<p>Movements change an envelope's credit on a date within the contract: a refill adds days (an amendment), an
adjustment removes some (a debt taken over). Each one has a comment, shown in the history.</p>

<h3 id="renewal">Renewal</h3>
<p><em>Renew</em> prepares the next contract from the day after the current one, with the same length, trackers and
envelopes; everything can be changed before saving. For each envelope with a balance, a box (unchecked by default)
carries the days left over, or deducts the overrun. Either becomes a movement on the new contract's first day.</p>
<p>The previous contract keeps its figures: carried days are not shown as consumption, and its chart stops on its last day.</p>

<h3 id="subprojects">Subprojects</h3>
<p>With <em>Include subprojects</em>, a contract also counts its subprojects' time. A subproject may still have
contracts of its own: on the same tracker and date, the closest contract wins. A subproject's report never shows its
parent's contract: its time covered by the parent appears as <em>Covered by the parent project</em>, without figures.</p>

<h3 id="report">Reading the report</h3>
<p>The contract buttons above the reports select what the whole page shows. Selections add up: all of them show every
logged hour once; one contract shows that contract's report; <em>Outside contract</em> shows the time no contract takes.
Each contract keeps its own balance: an overrun on one never uses another one's credit.</p>
<p>Credit figures count every hour charged to the contract, whoever reads them, private issues included; issue lists
still follow Redmine's permissions.</p>

<h3 id="sla">SLA delays</h3>
<p>With the SLA plugin configured for the project, the <em>Run &amp; Support (SLA)</em> report shows, for each SLA type
(response, resolution…), whether delays met their term. Reporting computes nothing itself: the time used (in business
hours of the SLA calendar) and the term of the issue's level and priority come from the SLA plugin, so figures are as
recent as its cache.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A delay <strong>ends</strong> when the issue last leaves the statuses in which it elapses (for a response delay,
  usually when it leaves <em>New</em>). It counts in the period where it ended; if the issue comes back to one of
  these statuses, the delay runs again and no longer counts as ended.</li>
  <li>A delay is <strong>respected</strong> when its time used does not exceed the term (100 % included), as in the SLA
  plugin's own <em>Respect</em> column.</li>
  <li><strong>Running delays</strong> are those of open issues in these statuses now. The SLA plugin keeps no history
  of time used, so they are only shown when the selected period includes today.</li>
</ul>
<p>Clicks open the matching issues, with the filters <em>SLA … : end of the delay</em> and <em>SLA … : time used
against the term</em>, which can also be used in any issue list.</p>
